## 10. Compare Both Approaches

| Feature          | Nested Loops            | HashSet                 |
| ---------------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Technique        | Compare every pair      | Store visited elements  |
| Time complexity  | O(n²)                   | O(n) average            |
| Auxiliary space  | O(1)                    | O(n)                    |
| Early stopping   | Yes, if duplicate found | Yes, if duplicate found |
| Extra collection | Not required            | HashSet                 |
| Performance      | Slower for large arrays | Generally faster        |

### Which approach is better?

**HashSet is generally better** for this problem because it reduces average time complexity from O(n²) to O(n).

However, nested loops use less extra memory.

---

## 11. Key Takeaways 🧠

- HashSet stores unique elements.
- `contains()` checks whether an element exists.
- `add()` inserts an element.
- Check before adding to detect duplicates.
- Average lookup and insertion time is O(1).
- HashSet approach takes O(n) average time and O(n) extra space.
- Learn brute force first, then optimize.

